    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A western Anatolian kingdom ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardis">Sardis</a>, which under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mermnad-dynasty">Mermnad dynasty</a> dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kizilirmak">Halys</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lydia">Lydia</a> struck the world’s earliest known coins from electrum carried by the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pactolus">Pactolus</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#croesus">Croesus</a>, famed for his wealth, attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> and lost his kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> around 546 BCE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lydia">Lydia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#media-region">Media</a> reportedly ended when a solar eclipse darkened a battle in 585 BCE, which Greek tradition said <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thales-of-miletus">Thales of Miletus</a> had predicted.</p></aside>
    </section>

