    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#homer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/homer.jpg" alt="Homer" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#homer">Homer</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homer_At_the_British_Museum_2024_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photograph by Mike Peel (www.mikepeel.net)., CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lydia">Lydia</a> lay in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a>, in the fertile valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gediz-river">Hermus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cayster-river">Cayster</a> rivers, inland from the Greek cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aegean">Aegean</a> coast. Its people spoke Lydian, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indo-european-languages">Indo-European</a> language of the Anatolian family related to Hittite and Luwian, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#homer">Homer</a> knew the region as Maeonia. Greek tradition, preserved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#herodotus">Herodotus</a>, held that a dynasty descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#heracles">Heracles</a> ruled Lydia for twenty-two generations, about 505 years, which would place its beginnings around 1200 BCE, but this early history is legendary and archaeology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardis">Sardis</a> shows only gradual growth after the collapse of Hittite power. The kingdom becomes historical with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gyges-of-lydia">Gyges</a>, who seized the throne around 680 BCE by killing the last Heraclid king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#candaules">Candaules</a>; Herodotus tells a famous tale of how Candaules’ wife made Gyges choose between death and regicide. Gyges founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mermnad-dynasty">Mermnad dynasty</a> and turned Lydia into an expanding power. Assyrian records call him Gugu of Luddi and report that he sought the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>, nomadic invaders from beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a> who had destroyed the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#phrygia">Phrygia</a>. He also sent rich gifts to the oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#apollo">Apollo</a> at Delphi, beginning the long Lydian relationship with the Greek sanctuaries.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sardis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sardis.jpg" alt="Sardis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sardis">Sardis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Bath-Gymnasium_complex_at_Sardis,_late_2nd_-_early_3rd_century_AD,_Sardis,_Turkey_(17098680002).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lydia">Lydia</a>’s wealth rested on fertile farmland, on its position on routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aegean">Aegean</a> and the Anatolian interior, and above all on gold. The river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pactolus">Pactolus</a>, flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardis">Sardis</a>, carried electrum, a natural alloy of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a>, and excavations at Sardis have uncovered a sixth-century workshop where gold was separated from silver. Lydia produced the earliest known coins in the late seventh century BCE: lumps of electrum of standard weight stamped with a lion’s head, probably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alyattes">Alyattes</a>. Later, separate pure gold and silver coins were struck, traditionally credited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#croesus">Croesus</a>, although some scholars date the first of these to the early Persian period. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#herodotus">Herodotus</a> also claimed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lydians">Lydians</a> were the first retail traders. Sardis was a large city with a fortified acropolis, a lower town of mud-brick houses and a massive outer wall. Religion combined Anatolian and Greek elements: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#artemis">Artemis</a> of Sardis and the mother goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cybele">Kybebe</a>, the later Cybele, were prominent, and the Lydian kings were generous patrons of Greek shrines. The monumental burial mounds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bin-tepe">Bin Tepe</a>, north of Sardis, include the tumulus of Alyattes, over 350 metres across, which Herodotus ranked among the wonders of the world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lydia">Lydia</a>’s military reputation rested on its cavalry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#herodotus">Herodotus</a> describes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lydians">Lydians</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#croesus">Croesus</a>’ day as the bravest and most warlike people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia-roman-province">Asia</a>, fighting on horseback with long spears. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mermnad-dynasty">Mermnad</a> kings used this force to expand against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greeks">Greeks</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gyges-of-lydia">Gyges</a> attacked Miletus and Smyrna and took the lower town of Colophon, but he died fighting the Cimmerians around 644 BCE, and under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ardys-of-lydia">Ardys</a> the invaders sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardis">Sardis</a> except for its citadel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alyattes">Alyattes</a> drove the Cimmerians out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, waged a long war against Miletus that ended in a treaty, and captured Smyrna around 600 BCE. He then fought a five-year war with the Median king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyaxares">Cyaxares</a>, which according to Herodotus ended in 585 BCE when an eclipse of the sun during a battle, said to have been predicted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thales-of-miletus">Thales of Miletus</a>, led both sides to make peace, with the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kizilirmak">Halys</a> as their frontier. Croesus attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ephesus">Ephesus</a> and brought all the Greek cities of the coast under tribute. His final war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> began with an indecisive battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-pteria">Pteria</a> in Cappadocia; Cyrus then followed him to Sardis, defeated the Lydian cavalry, according to Herodotus by frightening the horses with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#camel">camels</a>, and took the city after a short siege.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#gyges-of-lydia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gyges-of-lydia.jpg" alt="Gyges of Lydia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#gyges-of-lydia">Gyges of Lydia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Der_Ring_des_Gyges_(Ferrara_16_Jh).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Lydian state was a personal monarchy centred on the king and his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardis">Sardis</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mermnad-dynasty">Mermnad</a> kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gyges-of-lydia">Gyges</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ardys-of-lydia">Ardys</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sadyattes">Sadyattes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alyattes">Alyattes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#croesus">Croesus</a>, ruled for about 135 years, and succession seems to have passed from father to son, though Croesus had to overcome a half-brother supported by a rival faction at his accession around 560 BCE. Little is known of Lydian administration, since few Lydian texts survive and most information comes from Greek authors. The core territory was governed directly from Sardis, while conquered peoples were ruled more loosely. The Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ionia">Ionia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aeolis">Aeolis</a>, once subdued, kept their own institutions but paid tribute and provided troops, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#phrygians">Phrygians</a> and other peoples west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kizilirmak">Halys</a> acknowledged Lydian supremacy. Royal control of the gold supply and of coinage gave the kings unusual financial resources, which they used for monumental building, gifts to sanctuaries and diplomacy. Lydian kings married into neighbouring dynasties: Alyattes gave his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aryenis">Aryenis</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#astyages">Astyages</a>, son of the Median king, to seal the peace of 585 BCE. Croesus built a network of alliances with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#amasis-ii">Amasis of Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sparta">Sparta</a>, which he hoped would protect him against the rising power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#temple-of-artemis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis.jpg" alt="Temple of Artemis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#temple-of-artemis">Temple of Artemis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miniaturk_009.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">autor fotografie Zee Prime Original uploader was Zee Prime at cs.wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lydia">Lydia</a>’s most lasting contribution was coinage. The idea of stamped pieces of metal of guaranteed weight spread rapidly to the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ionia">Ionia</a> and then across the Greek world, and after the Persian conquest the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> adopted the bimetallic system of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> coins associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#croesus">Croesus</a>. Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greeks">Greeks</a>, coinage passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> and eventually to most of the world. Lydia also stood at the meeting point of Greek and Near Eastern culture. Its kings were among the first non-Greek rulers to patronise Greek sanctuaries on a large scale: Croesus dedicated gold and silver offerings at Delphi and paid for many of the columns of the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#temple-of-artemis">Temple of Artemis at Ephesus</a>, some bearing his name. The Lydian musical mode entered Greek music theory, and Greek writers credited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lydians">Lydians</a> with inventing games and luxuries. The story of Croesus, his wealth, his ambiguous oracle from Delphi and his fall, became one of the central moral tales of Greek literature, told by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#herodotus">Herodotus</a> and later writers, and the phrase “as rich as Croesus” survives in many languages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardis">Sardis</a> itself remained a major city under Persian, Hellenistic and Roman rule.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#cyrus-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great.jpg" alt="Cyrus the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyrus_II_(The_Great)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Surenae, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lydia">Lydia</a>’s fall came suddenly at the height of its power. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#astyages">Astyages</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#media-region">Media</a> around 550 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#croesus">Croesus</a>, Astyages’ brother-in-law, saw both a threat and an opportunity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#herodotus">Herodotus</a> reports that he consulted the oracle at Delphi and was told that if he attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> he would destroy a great empire; he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kizilirmak">Halys</a>, fought an indecisive battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-pteria">Pteria</a> and withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardis">Sardis</a> for the winter, expecting to renew the war with his allies in the spring. Cyrus instead pursued him at once, defeated the Lydian army outside Sardis and took the city, traditionally after a siege of fourteen days, around 546 BCE, although the precise year is debated. Croesus was captured; Greek tradition tells of his rescue from a pyre, while a Babylonian chronicle passage once read as recording his death is now considered too damaged to identify the country concerned. Lydia became the Achaemenid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#satrapy">satrapy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sparda">Sparda</a>, with Sardis as the western capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#achaemenid-empire">Persian Empire</a> and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#royal-road">Royal Road</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#susa">Susa</a>. A revolt led by the Lydian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pactyes">Pactyes</a> was soon crushed, and Lydian independence was never restored, although the language and culture survived into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lydia">Lydia</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardis">Sardis</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mermnad-dynasty">Mermnad</a> kings, dominated western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the seventh and sixth centuries BCE, struck what were probably the world’s first coins from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pactolus">Pactolus</a> electrum, bridged Greek and Near Eastern culture, and through the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#croesus">Croesus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyrus-the-great">Cyrus</a> in c. 546 BCE became a lasting Greek parable of wealth and fortune.</em></p>

